It's a bug exploit. (There's some debate about whether SC343-10 refers to 
this bug, or to a different bug which involves positively and negatively 
enchanted rings of gain constitution, which is much easier to abuse but 
which doesn't produce values nearly that high.)

The way it works: first, you get a huge stock of amulets of life saving 
(probably via pudding farming); then, you get HP and Pw that are 
significantly above average for your level (via any means, but if you're 
farming the amulets of life saving, you probably have plenty of potions 
of full healing and gain energy you can use for the purpose); then, you 
level-drain yourself to level 1 (probably most easily accomplished via 
the spell of drain life). Then you polymorph yourself, and choose to 
polymorph into your own race. This randomizes your stats slightly, and 
one of the stats it randomizes is your level. If your level reduces below 
1, then you die (that's what the amulets of life saving are for; they 
cause that cause to have no effect but blowing up the "oLS). If it stays 
the same, you just had your stats move around a bit. If it increases to 2 
or 3, though, your maximum HP/Pw double or triple respectively. Then when 
you drain yourself back down to level 1, they only reduce by a small 
amount (around equal to the amount you'd normally gain when going from 
level 1 to level 2). You can keep repeating this, doubling or tripling 
your max HP and Pw each time, until you have what you consider "enough". 
(Those values are probably enough for pretty much anything.)

Incidentally, this tends to leave your current HP/Pw rather lower than 
their max values, but that's nothing that can't be fixed using tins of 
nurse meat and confused scrolls of charging.

I haven't fixed this yet in NetHack 4 (unless one of the other people 
contributing patches has fixed it for me), but I'm planning to fix it by 
causing all sources of level loss to scale HP and Pw according to the 
ratio in levels. (That'd limit the exploits to draining to level 1 to get 
30 times the effect from a !oFH; not sure if that should be further fixed 
as well.)

